83
00:06:39,079 --> 00:06:45,018
Then it got down off the mushroom, and crawled away in the grass, merely remarking as it went,

84
00:06:45,019 --> 00:06:49,698
“One side will make you grow taller, and the other side

85
00:06:49,699 --> 00:06:53,837
will make you grow shorter.” “One side of what?

86
00:06:53,838 --> 00:06:58,218
The other side of what? thought Alice to herself.

87
00:06:58,219 --> 00:07:05,238
“Of the mushroom,” said the Caterpillar, just as if she had asked it aloud;

88
00:07:05,239 --> 00:07:09,198
and in another moment it was out of sight.

89
00:07:09,199 --> 00:07:13,638
Alice remained looking thoughtfully at the mushroom for a minute, trying to make

90
00:07:13,639 --> 00:07:18,499
out which were the two sides of it; and as it was perfectly round, she found this

91
00:07:18,500 --> 00:07:20,837
a very difficult question.

92
00:07:20,838 --> 00:07:25,817
However, at last she stretched her arms around it as far as they would go, and

93
00:07:25,818 --> 00:07:28,879
broke off a bit of the edge with each hand.

94
00:07:28,880 --> 00:07:32,959
“And now which is which?” she said to herself, and nibbled a little of the

95
00:07:32,960 --> 00:07:37,698
right hand bit to try the effect: the next moment she felt a violent blow

96
00:07:37,699 --> 00:07:41,297
underneath her chin: it had struck her foot!

97
00:07:41,298 --> 00:07:44,959
She was a good deal frightened by this very sudden change,

98
00:07:44,960 --> 00:07:50,058
but she felt that there was no time to be lost and she was shrinking rapidly; so

99
00:07:50,059 --> 00:07:54,738
she set to work at once to eat some of the other bit. Her chin was pressed so

100
00:07:54,739 --> 00:07:58,879
closely against her foot, that there was hardly room to open her mouth; but she

101
00:07:58,880 --> 00:08:05,658
did it at last, and managed to swallow a morsel of the left hand bit. “Come, my

102
00:08:05,659 --> 00:08:09,618
hands free at last!” said Alice in a tone of delight, which

103
00:08:09,619 --> 00:08:13,939
changed into alarm in another moment, when she found that her shoulders were

104
00:08:13,940 --> 00:08:18,259
nowhere to be found: all she could see, when she looked down, was an immense

105
00:08:18,260 --> 00:08:23,479
length of neck, which seemed to rise like a stalk out of a sea of green leaves

106
00:08:23,480 --> 00:08:29,718
that lay far below her. “What can all that green stuff be?” said Alice. “And where have

107
00:08:29,719 --> 00:08:33,018
my shoulders got to? And oh, my poor hands,

108
00:08:33,019 --> 00:08:36,259
how is it I can’t see you?” She was moving

109
00:08:36,260 --> 00:08:40,817
them about as she spoke, but no result seemed to follow, except a little shaking

110
00:08:40,818 --> 00:08:43,158
among the distant green leaves.

111
00:08:43,159 --> 00:08:47,779
As there seemed to be no chance of getting her hands up to her head, she

112
00:08:47,780 --> 00:08:52,098
tried to get her head down to them, and was delighted to find that her neck

113
00:08:52,099 --> 00:08:56,479
would bend about easily in any direction, like a serpent.

114
00:08:56,480 --> 00:09:01,638
She had just succeeded in curving it down into a graceful zigzag, and was

115
00:09:01,639 --> 00:09:06,317
going to dive in among the leaves, which she found to be nothing but the tops of

116
00:09:06,318 --> 00:09:11,959
the trees under which she had been wandering, when a sharp hiss made her draw back in

117
00:09:11,960 --> 00:09:19,279
a hurry: a large pigeon had flown into her face, and was beating her violently with its wings.

118
00:09:19,280 --> 00:09:27,638
“Serpent!” screamed the Pigeon. “I’m not a serpent!” said Alice indignantly. “Let me alone

119
00:09:27,639 --> 00:09:34,698
sir pentai say again!” repeated the Pigeon, but in a more subdued tone, and added

120
00:09:34,699 --> 00:09:40,698
with a kind of saw I’ve tried every way, and nothing seems to suit them!”

121
00:09:40,699 --> 00:09:45,317
“I haven’t the least idea what you’re talking about,” said Alice. “I’ve tried the

122
00:09:45,318 --> 00:09:50,178
roots of trees, and I’ve tried banks, and I’ve tried hedges,” the Pigeon went on,

123
00:09:50,179 --> 00:09:55,399
without attending to her; “but those serpents! There’s no pleasing them!” Alice

124
00:09:55,400 --> 00:09:59,357
was more and more puzzled, but she thought there was no use in saying

125
00:09:59,358 --> 00:10:02,178
