126
00:10:02,179 --> 00:10:07,279
“As if it wasn’t trouble enough hatching the eggs,” said the Pigeon; “but I must be

127
00:10:07,280 --> 00:10:12,018
on the lookout for serpents night and day! Why, I haven’t had a wink of sleep

128
00:10:12,019 --> 00:10:17,178
these three weeks!” “I’m very sorry you’ve been annoyed,” said

129
00:10:17,179 --> 00:10:21,919
Alice, who is beginning to see its meaning. “And just as I had taken the

130
00:10:21,920 --> 00:10:26,658
highest tree in the wood,” continued the Pigeon, raising its voice to a shriek,

131
00:10:26,659 --> 00:10:32,479
“and just as I was thinking I would be free of them at last, they must needs

132
00:10:32,480 --> 00:10:38,837
come wriggling down from the sky oh Serpent!” “But I’m not a serpent, I tell you!”

133
00:10:38,838 --> 00:10:43,578
said Alice. “I’m a— —I’m a—” “Well! What are you

134
00:10:43,579 --> 00:10:51,078
cried the Pigeon. “I can see you’re trying to invent something!” “I— —I’m a little girl,”

135
00:10:51,079 --> 00:10:55,759
said Alice, rather doubtfully, as she remembered the number of changes she had

136
00:10:55,760 --> 00:10:57,439
gone through that day.

137
00:10:57,440 --> 00:11:03,919
“A likely story indeed!” said the Pigeon in a tone of deepest contempt. “I’ve seen a

138
00:11:03,920 --> 00:11:09,439
good many little girls in my time, but never one with such a neck as that! No, no!

139
00:11:09,440 --> 00:11:13,817
You’re a serpent; and there’s no use to denying it. I suppose you’ll be telling

140
00:11:13,818 --> 00:11:19,698
me next that you've never tasted an egg!” “I have tasted eggs, certainly,” said Alice,

141
00:11:19,699 --> 00:11:24,558
who was a very truthful child; “but little girls eat eggs quite as much as serpents

142
00:11:24,559 --> 00:11:29,058
